  "Question": "If \\(\\dfrac1x-\\dfrac1y=\\dfrac1z,\\) then \\(z\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(y-x\\)",
    "B": "\\(x-y\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{y-x}{xy}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{xy}{y-x}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{xy}{x-y}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The original fractions require \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) to be nonzero. Combining the left side over the common denominator \\(xy\\) gives a single fraction equal to \\(1/z\\):\n\\[\n\\frac1z=\\frac1x-\\frac1y=\\frac{y-x}{xy}.\n\\]\nSince \\(1/z\\ne0\\), we also have \\(y-x\\ne0\\). Both sides are therefore nonzero, so taking reciprocals is valid and gives\n\\[\nz=\\frac{xy}{y-x}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice D.",
